Hello.

I have an environment where most of my hosts use kernel 2.4, and I'm
testing a new host with kernel 2.6 on this environment. Both hosts run
the same software, it was only ported to the more recent O.S. The
software is POP3/IMAP, the mailboxes are in NFS, so that it reads a lot
from the eth1 network interface. That interface is used only for NFS.

I noticed that the host that has kernel 2.6 (smtp-fe14) has a much
higher network traffic than the other 2.4 hosts (smtp-fe01 up to
smtp-fe13). You can see it on the MRTG attached. The gray line is the
inbound traffic at smtp-fe14's network interface used for NFS only. It
reaches around 80-90Mbps, while the other hosts use around 60Mbps.

I use only NFSv3, and all accesses are done on NFS with non-root user.

So I ask: Is this behaviour expected? Did someone else notice that on
kernel 2.6? Can this be affecting my storage, for example, increasing
the RPC's that are being done in the server? (I don't have how to check
it, because this host is responsible for only a small part of RPC's on
the server, and I can't really see such a difference on total of RPC's
on the server). Can this be related to prefetch? How can I tune NFS
prefetch in a kernel 2.6?

Thanks a lot,
Filipe

